[{:seon.task/name "with-merge-mg-check-cost"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.check/mg-check` prints each checked contract's mg/check ms (100 runs, seed 42). Measured (load about 10): of the 82 contracts' ~130 s, `seon.template/with-merge` alone takes 57 s (570 ms a run), then `seon.task/cycle-in` 15 s, `seon.proofs/failures` 11.7 s and `seon.proofs/costs` 11.2 s; the median contract is well under 1 s, so with-merge sets the floor of a sharded mg/check (4 shards: 94 s wall). with-merge is one pass over its declarations, so the cost is in generating or checking `[:vector :seon.task/declaration]` (the generator's size, the instrumented contract validating every nested declaration in and out, or `seon.task/task-id` and `seon.task/default-branch`'s own contracts per element). Find which by measuring each part (generate only, validate only, call uninstrumented vs instrumented) and fix it at its cause: a schema whose generator is too large gets a bounded :gen; a contract check that repeats work does it once. Proof: a `seon.cost` proof that with-merge plus its contract check grows linearly in the declarations and costs no more per declaration than one task-id and one default-branch call (from `seon.cost` units), at three sizes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.template-test/with-merge-checks-in-its-share}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-request-is-one-linked-transaction"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (merged; DECISIONS: the owner's 2026-10-22 answers on requests, failed tasks and task content; the archived paper DESIGN-tasks.md (git history) D2(a), D3(a), D4, D5(a)):\n- `my.root/request!` makes a request (ns, name, plan, optional proofs and model) one task that designs, builds and merges itself (`seon.template/request`, the default); for an experiment it takes `seon.template/design-build` (its design-build task, kind :work, followed by its merge task on its branch, linked by :needs: `seon.template/with-merge`) or `seon.template/stages` (design -> implement -> merge on design's branch) in its place. `my.root/request!` writes `seon.task/create-tx` and `seon.task/execution-tx` (`seon.template/request-tx`) in one `seon.root/transact-main!`, and the reconciler forks only from a committed main, so every agent of the set starts from the same graph.\n- The task rides the writer's :spawn and fork messages to `seon.agent/init!`; `seon.turn/current-task` reads it (`[:turn :task]` deleted), so `my.task/current` is known from an agent's first read.\n- An unrun proof refuses done as :proof-unknown, not :proof-red (`seon.reconcile/done-explain`), as v1 counted it.\n- A failed task is held for root: `my.task/fail!` writes `:seon.task/failure` {:reason :counterexample :run} on the task on its branch and ends the loop (outcome :failed); the judge carries this run's failure onto main with the task held; `seon.reconcile/task-state` derives :failed; `seon.problems/report` has a :failed class; a release (`my.root/run!`) is root's answer. Deleted: `my.task/send-back!` (a turn stop the judge refined into an automatic retry).\n- Task content reaches main at the run's end: `seon.task/carry-tx` compares base, branch and main over `seon.merge/carried-platform-attrs`; a branch-only change overwrites main's, and a two-sided different edit is recorded on the task (`:seon.task/task-conflicts`, the :task-conflict class of `seon.problems/report`) for root. The writer's :branch-db read answers the merge-base db. Deleted: `seon.task/plan-tx`.\nOpen (root's), for the staged experiment: proofs still accumulate along :needs, and a design step's new proofs reach main only as the plan's text, so an implement step whose request carried no proofs is refused :no-proofs by root's judge."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.template-test/a-design-build-request-is-its-design-build-task-and-its-merge
                      seon.template-test/the-stages-are-design-implement-merge-on-one-branch
                      seon.template-test/a-request-is-one-transaction-and-forks-after-it
                      seon.reconcile-test/done-explain-table
                      seon.agent-test/a-forked-agent-knows-its-task
                      seon.reconcile-test/a-task-marked-failed-is-held-for-root
                      seon.agent-test/a-failure-marked-through-the-server-holds-the-task
                      seon.reconcile-test/a-branch-carries-every-task-it-changed-and-records-a-two-sided-edit}}
 {:seon.task/name "templates-are-held-task-data"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the templates are strings in `seon.template/instructions`, keyed by `:seon.task/kind`; `seon.template/request` copies :whole's, `seon.template/design-build` :work's into its design-build task and `with-merge` :merge's into its merge, and `seon.template/stages` copies :design's and :implement's into its stages; `seon.task/accumulate` hard-codes which members flow, and proofs accumulate along :needs (docs/tasks.md#a-request-is-one-transaction says this is unsettled). Owner, 2026-10-22 (DECISIONS): templates are held tasks in resources/seon/tasks, loaded by `my.root/load-tasks!`; `:seon.task/owned` on each member's schema says what carries; proofs are owned, not carried; design writing implement's proofs is seon.task/design-writes-the-implement-steps-proofs; `:seon.task/kind` goes, and the attributes present decide (a merge carries :seon.task/target). Deletes: `seon.template/instructions`, the join in `seon.task/accumulate`, :seon.task/kind."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.task/design-writes-the-implement-steps-proofs"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.template-test/a-template-is-a-held-task seon.task-test/owned-members-carry-by-schema-property seon.task-test/proofs-never-accumulate}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-design-writes-its-proof-first"
  :seon.task/plan "Pipeline 1 (the archived paper live-2026-10-25.md (git history) F4): the two printer designs reproduced their bug by turn 11 (`:id` under the `#` of `#:seon.task{`; a keyword past a cut), then read seon.print one function a turn to the cap and registered no proof; one wrote the fix's body, which the design leaves to implement. The design instructions (```seon.template/instructions```) said to read what the plan touches and try the idea, then write the proofs: reading came first, with no end. Now they say proof first: reproduce what the plan says is wrong as a deftest in its test namespace, register it, run it red, then read what the change touches. Second cause, from the same runs: a guessed name's source pointed at `(source ns)`, which answered the ns form alone; the agent took it for all that was recorded and spent five turns finding the namespace's private functions. ```my.agent/source-fn``` of a namespace now answers its ns form, then every definition a line (```seon.doc/definition-lines``` of ```seon.ref/namespace-view```, the lines a namespace's citation shows), and ```my.agent/not-found``` points there. Replaces the ns-form-only answer; no new mechanism."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.template-test/each-steps-instructions-are-its-task-briefly-prose-first seon.repl-test/an-agent-repl-answers-what-a-clojure-repl-answers}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-finding-is-a-task"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-30: \"I want to have the focus on fewer and smaller repl context. So we mine the system for long parts and make tasks to shrink them since we can write the proofs for this along with improved perf requirements. So the system shrinks itself\"; \"I think we can write a template to auto generate the tasks based on the code we have indexed right?\"; tests are production code (\"Isn't test code also production code?\"). What exists: the db indexes every form's source (src and test alike: :form/src) and every wiki section's text and lead; seon.proofs/per-call (my.agent/cost) holds each contracted function's measured ticks, bytes and calls per call from the cached test verdicts; seon.problems' classes report findings as open problem rows, judged on what a branch changed (:uncited-var); seon.template/stall turns one finding (a stall) into a task. Measured 2026-10-30 on main: src 2,080 forms (1.9 MB), 56 over 4 KB and 11 over 8 KB (seon.ref/scan 19 KB, seon.reconcile/advance 14 KB, seon.program/reach-step 13 KB); test 2,040 forms (2.0 MB), 35 over 4 KB; wiki sections up to 34 KB (docs/forks.md's jolt, docs/source.md's tests run by reach 24 KB).
The design: one mechanism, a finding becomes a task. (1) The finding is a problem class (it finds, and closes when the condition goes, so a merged shrink cannot grow back): :long-form, a live form of src or test over :seon.config.shrink/form-tokens; :long-section, a section over :seon.config.shrink/section-chars; later kinds by the same path (a lead too long or too short; a function whose measured per-call cost is far over its estimate; a flaky test, task seon.proofs/a-flaky-test-is-a-task). Each judged on what a branch changed, like :uncited-var, so a branch cannot add one, and once over main to find what exists. (2) The template per kind (seon.template/finding): a problem row becomes a task declaration, one per row key (its name), its plan pre-filled from the row and the index: what to shrink, its size now and the bound, the vars it defines, the tests reaching it and its measured per-call ticks now; its proofs: the tests reaching it stay green, the row closes (the size under the bound), and its measured ticks no worse than now. (3) Root writes the task the first time it sees the row open, released for a Seon agent (largest first); the reconciler admits them by :seon.config.agent/max-running as any task. The owner and the session review the merges.
Proofs: the template answers a declaration whose plan names the size, bound, tests and ticks (on the world: a form over the bound); a branch growing a form past the bound has the open row; root writes one task per open row and none for a closed one."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.template-test/a-long-form-becomes-a-task-that-names-its-size-tests-and-ticks
                      seon.problems-test/a-form-over-the-bound-is-a-long-form-until-it-is-split}}]
